WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.920
AI-assisted web application assessment means using AI to support authorized security review, not to replace the assessor or operate outside approved boundaries.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:19.380
In a professional assessment, AI can help organize information, explain unfamiliar concepts, summarize notes, draft report language, and reason through possible vulnerability classes.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.880
The human assessor remains responsible for scope, evidence, conclusions, and client communication.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.260
This distinction matters because web application testing is context-heavy.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:40.200
A finding depends on the application purpose, user roles, data sensitivity, business logic, authentication model, authorization boundaries, and the rules of engagement.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:47.780
AI can help process that context, but it does not automatically know what is authorized, what is important to the client, or what evidence is sufficient.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.680
Useful AI support often appears in planning, documentation, triage, and reporting.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:01:04.860
For example, an assistant can turn rough assessment notes into a checklist, summarize a long proxy history, help compare a finding draft to a template, or explain why a control might reduce risk.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.880
Those outputs should be treated as working material for analyst review, not as confirmed results.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:17.940
AI should not be trusted to run unsupervised testing, bypass scope limits, make final severity decisions, or publish findings without review.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.960
It can misunderstand application behavior, hallucinate, or suggest unsafe actions.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:30.300
A strong workflow keeps authorization, scope, evidence, data protection, and human judgment at the center.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.560
AI is useful when it helps the assessor think more clearly and document more consistently.
